Implementation of `rename`:
1. Read the file at `dir/{old_id}.note`. If it doesn't exist → `std::io::Error` (
NotFound).
2. Parse it: splitn(4, SEP). If it's not a valid 4-part format, probably return an
 error.
3. Reconstruct the content with the new id and new title, and the same pinned flag
 and body.
4. Atomically: write the new file `dir/{new_id}.note`, then delete the old file. To
 be careful about the "no trace under the old id" requirement — write first, then
 delete the old one. If the new id already exists, writing will overwrite it — that
's fine.

Edge case: what if old_id == new_id? In that case, just rewrite the file in place
 (delete the same file after writing — but writing and then deleting would lose the
 note!). Handle this: if old == new, only update the title.

Let's write the function:

```rust
pub fn rename(dir: &Path, old_id: &str, new_id: &str, new_title: &str) -> std::io
::Result<()> {
    let old_path = dir.join(format!("{}.note", old_id));
    let content = fs::read_to_string(&old_path)?;
    let parts: Vec<&str> = content.splitn(4, SEP).collect();
    if parts.len() != 4 {
        return Err(std::io::Error::new(
            std::io::ErrorKind::InvalidData,
            "malformed note file",
        ));
    }
    let (title, pinned_flag, body) = (parts[1], parts[2], parts[3]);
    let new_content = format!(
        "{}{}{}{}{}{}{}",
        new_id, SEP, new_title, SEP, pinned_flag, SEP, body
    );
    let new_path = dir.join(format!("{}.note", new_id));
    fs::write(&new_path, &new_content)?;
    if old_path != new_path {
        fs::remove_file(&old_path)?;
    }
    Ok(())
}
```

Note: `load_all` reads all files in the directory and doesn't filter by the `.note
` extension, but that's the existing behavior so it's fine.

Also, note that `load_all` reads every entry including subdirectories — `read_to_string
` on a directory should fail. That's an existing issue and not my concern.

